{
  "passage": "Combustion is a chemical process in which a substance reacts with oxygen to give off heat. A substance that can burn is called a combustible substance or a fuel. For combustion to happen, three things are needed together: a fuel, air to supply oxygen, and enough heat to raise the fuel to its ignition temperature. The ignition temperature is the lowest temperature at which a substance catches fire. Kerosene has a low ignition temperature, so it catches fire easily, while wood must be heated much more. Removing any one of these three things puts a fire out, and this is the idea behind every fire extinguisher. Water is the most common extinguisher. It cools the burning material below its ignition temperature, and the steam it produces also helps to keep air away. But water should never be used on fires involving electrical equipment, because water conducts electricity and can give a shock. Water is also not suitable for oil and petrol fires, because the oil floats on water and keeps burning. For such fires, carbon dioxide is the best extinguisher. Being heavier than air, it forms a blanket around the fire, cutting off the supply of oxygen, and it does not harm electrical equipment. Covering a small fire with a blanket or sand works in the same way.",
